## 1. A physical two-branch preparation

Work on a finite simple cubic torus with even periods at least six. Use
Gauss law div E=q-1_A and store every edge from A to B. All A sites are
occupied. Let

    a=(0,0,0), d=(1,1,0), h=(2,2,0),
    c=(1,0,0), e=(0,1,0), b=(-1,0,0),
    v1=(0,-1,0), v2=(0,0,1), v3=(0,0,-1).

The six neighbors of a are b,c,e,v1,v2,v3. Set q_a=+1, q_d=q_h=-1,
and every other A charge +1. Occupy v1,v2,v3 with charge +1. There
are two matter words: in m_c, c has charge +1 and e is empty; in m_e,
e has charge +1 and c is empty. Site b and every other B site are empty.
Both words have four occupied B sites and total charge equal to |A|.
Thus N=N0+4 with N0=|A|, and the zero-total Gauss constraint is consistent.

For each oriented A-to-B link let U_ab raise its electric coordinate by
one. A positive-charge hop A to B uses U_ab^{-1}. Choose an integer
flow E_com with divergence -1 at d, -2 at h, +1 at each v_i, and zero
elsewhere. Such a fixed finite flow exists on the connected graph: send
one unit of positive charge from d to v1 and one from h to each of v2,v3
along fixed paths. Put V_com=product_e U_e^(E_com,e).

For any divergence-free field vector phi define

    J_c phi=|m_c> V_com U_dc^{-1} phi,
    J_e phi=|m_e> V_com U_de^{-1} phi,
    J_-=(J_c-J_e)/sqrt(2).                                  (1)

Each term separately satisfies the full Gauss law: the extra link shift
adds divergence -1 at d and +1 at c or e. At d the combined divergence
is -2, at h it is -2, and at the four occupied B sites it is +1.
All other deviations from the all-A-plus background vanish. The two
matter words are orthogonal, so J_- is an isometry. The strings encode
the required field/matter correlation and are part of the supplied
preparation. No charged matter superposition with an unchanged field
is substituted for (1).

Take the actual original selected resolved mark j=(a,b,sigma), sigma=+1
or -1 fixed, with B_j=P j_(a,b,sigma) F_a P. This is the same instrument
as in the common-limit parent. The old record at a has charge +1.

## 2. Exact single-mark effect

On m_c the only legal outward destinations of F_a are e and b. The b
term is killed by the subsequent formation at b because b is then
occupied. The only retained path goes a to e. On m_e, similarly, the
only retained path goes a to c. All v_i are occupied and blocked.
The two paths have precisely the same final matter word: a has sigma,
b has -sigma, and c,e,v1,v2,v3 all have charge +1. Other charges agree.
Consequently

    B_j J_- phi = |m_out> V_com U_ab^sigma
                   (U_ae^{-1}U_dc^{-1}-U_ac^{-1}U_de^{-1})phi/sqrt(2).

All link unitaries commute. The relative product of the two routes is
the plaquette Wilson shift W_p or its adjoint, depending on orientation.
Thus exactly on the whole divergence-free input field space,

    J_-* B_j* B_j J_-=I-(W_p+W_p*)/2.                       (2)

The effect is positive and bounded by 2I. The common flow cancels. The
identity is independent of the selected resolved sign. Summing the two
signs coherently multiplies this initial effect by two because their
final A/b charge words are orthogonal; no positive-time equivalence of
the complete instruments follows from that statement.

At a flat link-angle configuration W_p=1 the two amplitudes cancel.
For a normalized fluctuating quantum field, the vacuum expectation is
generally nonzero. Calling the flat-configuration interference “dark”
must not be confused with a detector that never clicks on a quantum
vacuum. If the two initial matter branches are dephased, their two
effects add incoherently and (2) becomes I: the field dependence is lost.
This dephasing is not part of the original instrument or preparation.

The all-B-empty first effect 5I is not contradicted. Equation (1)
prepares four occupied B sites and two flipped A signs in a coherent
physical state; it lies outside that initial matter sector.

## 3. Prepared field signal at time zero

On the divergence-free reference field in (1), use normalized compact
weak-field vacuum and one-particle Hermite packets. They are a way to
specify initial states, not a theorem that the charged matter sector
evolves by the old vacuum Hamiltonian. Keep the full h=KD+delta H4 after
time zero. Define the same plaquette reference variance v_p>0 and mode
overlap chi_p as in the pinned packet/readout arguments, evaluated at
this preparation time. Set K=g^2/(2tau_*), delta=1/(4tau_*g^2), with
tau_*=a/c fixed.

The initial rate of the selected original mark is kappa times (2).
The reference characteristic functions and exponentially small cutoff
tails give

    r_0=kappa[1-exp(-g^2 v_p/2)]+O(kappa exp(-c0/g^2)),
    r_1-r_0=kappa g^2 exp(-g^2v_p/2)|chi_p|^2
                                      +O(kappa exp(-c0/g^2)). (3)

In particular r_0/(kappa g^2)->v_p/2 and
r_1/(kappa g^2)->v_p/2+|chi_p|^2. The one-particle rate excess is positive
if chi_p!=0. The ratio of that excess to the vacuum rate has a finite
nonzero limit; lowering g alone does not remove the vacuum background.
This is a quadratic-field interference probe with a supplied matter
preparation, not an absorption coupling or an energy balance theorem.

## 4. A conservative positive-window statement using the full dynamics

Let Psi_n,g=J_- phi_n,g for n=0,1. These physical smooth initial vectors
belong to D(D). The fixed integer strings shift electric derivatives by
bounded constants. Rescaling the finite-Hermite packet at time zero gives

    ||D Psi_n,g||<=C g^-2,
    ||h Psi_n,g||<=C/(tau_*g^2),
    ||B_j Psi_n,g||<=C g.                                  (4)

The D bound uses its actual charge-dependent quadratic polynomial, not
an assumption of electric confinement or vacuum dynamics in this sector.
The H4 term is bounded at fixed graph and is multiplied by delta. No
claim that the supplied matter state is an H4 eigenstate is required.

With U(s)=exp(-ihs), the domain estimate yields
||[U(s)-I]Psi_n,g||<=C s/(tau_*g^2). Expand the squared norm of B_j U(s)Psi
around B_j Psi. The initially small output in (4) gives

    |<B_j*B_j>_U(s)-<B_j*B_j>_0|
       <=C[s/(tau_*g)+s^2/(tau_*^2 g^4)].                  (5)

The bounded global dissipator comparison, directly from Duhamel, is
||T_kappa(s)-U_s||_(1->1)<=2 kappa G s, where
G=sum_m||B_m||^2. This is the fixed-graph estimate also reconstructed
in the unrestricted-count independent PRE. All background marks are
retained; the comparison has a graph-dependent constant.

Let N_j([0,b]) count every selected mark in that positive interval with
all other marks unrestricted. Its exact mean is

    m_n=kappa int_0^b Tr[B_j*B_j T_kappa(s)|Psi_n,g><Psi_n,g|] ds.

Equations (3)–(5) imply, at fixed graph and kappa tau_*>0,

    m_n=kappa b [g^2 q_n+O(g^4)
        +O(b/(tau_*g)+b^2/(tau_*^2g^4)+kappa G b)],        (6)
    q_0=v_p/2, q_1=v_p/2+|chi_p|^2.

One sufficient window is b=o(tau_*g^3), b>0. It gives

    m_n/(kappa b g^2)->q_n,
    (m_1-m_0)/(kappa b g^2)->|chi_p|^2.                   (7)

Here kappa stays fixed. Indeed b/(tau_*g^3), b^2/(tau_*^2g^6) and
kappa G b/g^2 all tend to zero. The time resolution becomes finer;
the result makes no assertion about a fixed-duration detection window,
long prior propagation, or signal survival before this prepared time.
The g^3 choice is a sufficient consequence of a coarse domain bound,
not an optimal detector bandwidth or a necessary exponent.

