Put lambda_j=kappa||B_j||^2, a full-space norm rather than the small
prepared initial rate. The ordered two-selected-event intensity is at
most lambda_j^2 by positivity, trace preservation and bounded marked
maps. Hence

    0<=E[N_j(N_j-1)]<=lambda_j^2 b^2,
    max(0,m_n-m_n^2)<=Var(N_j)<=m_n+lambda_j^2b^2-m_n^2.

Under (7), lambda_j^2b^2/m_n=O(kappa b/g^2)->0. Thus Var(N_j)/m_n->1.
This is derived from the factorial bound; no Poisson or Bernoulli law
is imposed on the actual record count.

With N independent supplied preparations per arm, chi_p!=0, and exact
control of the two preparation laws, the squared signal-to-standard-
error ratio for their sample-mean difference is

    SNR^2 ~ N kappa b g^2 |chi_p|^4/(v_p+|chi_p|^2).        (8)

For b=tau_*g^(3+zeta), zeta>0, fixed kappa tau_*, a sufficient repetition
order is g^(-5-zeta), with a sufficiently large constant for a specified
Chebyshev sign-error bound. This cost excludes creating the coherent
matter/field preparation, resetting it, timing it or delivering a packet.
It is not a resource advantage claim for an implemented experiment.

